hypotheses.statement:
For finite-index subgroups $A\leq\Gamma$ and $B\leq\Lambda$, an isomorphism $\delta:A\to B$, and a nonzero finite-dimensional projective unitary representation $\sigma$ satisfying $$\label{eq:intro-multiplier}
 \sigma(a)\sigma(b)
 =\frac{\mu(a,b)}{\omega(\delta(a),\delta(b))}\sigma(ab),
 \qquad \sigma(e)=I,$$ define $$\label{eq:intro-model}
 E(A,B,\delta,\sigma)
 =L^2(M)\,\overline\otimes_{L_{\mu|_A}(A)}
       \bigl(V_\sigma\otimes L^2(N)\bigr),
 \qquad M=L_\mu(\Gamma),\quad N=L_\omega(\Lambda).$$

outputs.exact_statement:
For a matched isolated pair the homogeneous dimensions agree, $Q_S=Q_T=Q$. There is a family of positive injective self-adjoint operators $b_x$, affiliated to $\mathcal A_1$, for every $x\in B_S$, which strongly commute with $P_x$ and are invariant under the complementary source factors. They satisfy the exact identities $$\label{eq:scale-isolated-height-covariance}
 \sigma_a(b_x)=\operatorname{dil}(a,x)^{-Q}b_{ax},\qquad
 \beta_h(b_x)=\operatorname{dil}(h^{-1},P_x)^{-Q}b_x.$$ These are the inverse angular-Jacobian laws. The same construction passes to the normal copies used in the amplified models.

For every selected tuple position $i$, $$\label{eq:input-16}
 2\rho_T(s_i e_{p(i)})=2\rho_S(e_i),\qquad
 \mu(h^{-1})=\sum_{i\in I}s_it_i e_{p(i)}+O_{\mathrm{tight}}(1).$$ These assertions hold with the total parameters on the further offset limits specified above.

Let $B_S,B_T$ be the source and target chamber spaces with their compact probabilities. There is a constant $0<c_0<\infty$ and an isometry $$\label{eq:input-22}
 \begin{split}
 V:L^2(B_T;\mathcal H)&\longrightarrow L^2(B_S;\mathcal H),\\
 (V(f\otimes\xi))(Z)&=c_0^{-1/2}D_Z^{-1/2}P_Z(f)\xi.
 \end{split}$$ The expression on the second line is initially interpreted through positive form integrals and then extended by the stated isometry.
